Japanese Ambassador to Cambodia confirms to Minister Vongsey Visoth that Cambodia will receive more official development financing from Japan
This statement was made during a courtesy call and discussion with Mr. Vongsey Visoth, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Economy and Finance on June 19, 2023 to strengthen and expand bilateral cooperation. The party between Cambodia and Japan for the benefit of both countries.
He added that Japan is a good, close and loyal friend of Cambodia in all circumstances. Cambodia and Japan have a long-standing relationship, and 2023 marks the 70th anniversary of diplomatic ties between our two countries. At present, the relationship and cooperation between the two countries have been constantly strengthened and expanded, and have been upgraded to a comprehensive strategic partnership.
Mr. Vongsey Visoth expressed his deep gratitude to the Government of Japan and the people of Japan for their contribution to the socio-economic development and development of Cambodia from the stage of seeking peace to the stage of rehabilitation and development of Cambodia through the provision of development assistance. Both financing and technical as well as direct investment (FDI).
Mr. Vongsey Visoth also thanked the Government of Japan for helping to establish the Cambodia-Japan Digital Production Center in accordance with the human resource development strategy of the Royal Government of Cambodia, which is paying close attention to and promoting this sector.
He also requested the Japanese side to continue to help develop human capital in Cambodia, especially the training of quality skills and response to the needs of Cambodian industry and capacity building and development in the public sector to make the Cambodian public sector more capable, more Smart and clean.
On that occasion, Mr. Vongsey Visoth and Japanese Ambassador UENO Atsushi also discussed about the investment of Japanese businessmen and investors in Cambodia. The two sides also exchanged views on participating in improving the investment and business environment in Cambodia, as well as the desire of Japanese investors and businessmen to attract and encourage more investment in Cambodia.
However, it remains to be seen whether the two countries can resolve their differences, many of which have implications for international finance, security and stability.
Apart from the willingness to discuss, there are few signs that they are preparing to change their stubborn stance on issues ranging from trade to Taiwan and the human rights situation in China and Hong Kong and the South China Sea and the war. Russia in Ukraine and so on.
