Red Plus project in the southern Cardamom mountain range creates new jobs and changes the lives of people in O'Som commune, Veal Veng district, Pursat province
(Pursat): The Red Plus South Cardamom Mountains project, implemented by the Wildlife Alliance (WA), has helped improve the lives of people living in Chhay Lok village, O'Som commune, Veal Veng district, Pursat province. Raise animals.
The project aims to provide people living around natural resource protection areas with new occupations to feed their families by cutting off their old occupations, logging and hunting. This project has enabled them to raise chickens and frogs for distribution in the market.
At the same time, the community in Chhay Lok village has also become an agricultural model village in the Cardamom Mountains and is a hope for the development of other villages or communities through this project in the future.
He said that in this project, not only provided technology to local people in Chhay Lok village, but the staff of the organization regularly visited to check the actual situation of livestock. In addition to providing them with breeds of chickens, frogs and some animal feed.
Ms. Samban Kolap, a resident of Chhay Lok village, O'Som commune, Veal Veng district, Pursat province, said that through the Red Plus project, she decided to raise chickens. Have an expert come down to take care of when the chicken is sick.
"The new business of raising chickens and farming at home is the best job for my family because it can earn a living," said a former farmer who has been earning a living since she was a child. Income for the family and we do not have to go far to work for them. Now I am at home taking care of my children, I do not have to worry like before. ”
Hong Heng, a 62-year-old widow who volunteered to raise chickens through the REDD + project, said: "Rest, but now turned to raising chickens, see that not only can have more time to rest, but also provide a better livelihood."
She received support from the REDD + project to breed 10 hens, 2 males, 9 bags of chicken feed and some chicks. Over the past nine months, her chickens have grown steadily and have also been sold for daily use.
