WASHINGTON (Reuters) - Despite US war on Russia, the United States will not make special arrangements for Ukraine to become a member of NATO, said US President Joe Biden on Saturday. This was released by the AFP news agency on Sunday, June 18, 2023.
In the past, NATO members in Eastern Europe have been pushing for better plans for Ukraine's accession, but key allies such as the United States and Germany have been reluctant to move beyond. The only promise is that Ukraine will be able to join NATO one day in the future.
NATO has already supplied tens of billions of dollars worth of weapons to Ukraine since Russia launched its invasion of neighboring countries in February 2022. However, some NATO leaders are concerned that accepting Ukraine as a member will increase the likelihood that NATO will face Russia directly in the war.
In April, NATO acquired its 31st member, Finland, while Sweden's application for membership continued to be strained by Turkey, with Ankara accusing Sweden of providing asylum to Kurdish militants it considers. Terrorists.
