She explained: “There are usually two types of raising ducks in Cambodia: raising in cages and raising in the field. "In the dry season, most egg-raising farmers start selling hens, so domestic production of duck eggs is reduced while demand is high, which is why the price of duck eggs starts to rise."
She notes that the price of duck eggs may start to fall during July after farmers start buying hens to produce duck eggs for sale.
She said that at present, the retail price of duck eggs in the market has increased by 700 riel per egg and the wholesale price is more than 500 riel per egg.
She added that while the price of duck eggs rises, such prices could affect consumers and the market in the future.
The general director of Pong Tear Khmer village said: "Consumers are paying their prices, if we look at the current economy, there are financial problems, if the traders do not understand the buyers. "Because now a duck egg is 700, so it is expensive, which can make consumers turn to eat something else."
For her farm, she supplies about 20,000 duck eggs a day in the market in Phnom Penh and some provinces at the same price without any increase. However, she understands that not enough is needed.
A vegetable seller at Borey Piphop Thmey Market said that duck eggs, which used to sell for 400 or 500 riel per egg, have risen to 700 riel per egg, and the price has risen for more than half a month. However, she said, buyers continue to buy normally, only they pay more.
As the price of chicken and duck eggs in Cambodia rises, prices also rise due to a shortage of farm supplies and rising export demand. According to the release of the Bangkok Post.
