SEOUL (Reuters) - South Korea extracted parts of a rocket used in North Korea's first military spy satellite launch that failed in May. This was released by Reuters on Friday, June 16, 2023.
The South Korean military said on Friday that fragments of the rocket had been recovered from the sea on Thursday, while experts were continuing to search for more rocket fragments. On May 31, North Korea launched one of its first military reconnaissance satellites, but failed, with both satellites and rockets crashing into the sea.
South Korea also found fragments of a North Korean rocket off its west coast shortly after the crash. According to the South Korean military, the rocket fragments recovered on Thursday are likely to be the second part of the rocket and will continue to search for satellite warheads and the third part of the rocket.
