Cambodian Aquaculture Association applauds eel export project to China
BEIJING (Xinhua) - The Chinese embassy in Cambodia has recently updated its "Registration Information for Imported Food Producers" list, saying it is the country's first natural fisheries exporter. Cambodia has completed its inspection and approval by the Customs Administration, and the company has been successfully registered in the "Registration Information for Imported Food Producers" system.
In relation to this news, Mr. Sok Raden, President of the Council of Cambodian Aquatic Farmers Association, told Radio Cambodia-China Friendship on June 14, 2023 that this is good news and proud because China is a huge market.
"So we are grateful for this good news, and I, as one of the representatives of the Hydropower Association, represent the breeders, especially we are pleased with the first opportunity and look forward to other products in the field." "Aquaculture may be brought to the Chinese market due to the large Chinese market, so we are happy to hear that on the Chinese side, the Chinese people, as well as the Chinese state, give us the opportunity to be able to export our products is a great hope."
He said that after hearing this news, he also met with farmers to be ready to think about the techniques of raising these products in accordance with the standards that the Chinese side wants to accelerate exports. "It is not difficult to raise snails, raise snails, eels, we can do it in a natural way, because in our district there is good potential, good soil, good natural resources, our land, our water in the district is not contaminated with many chemicals."
However, he also hopes that the Ministry of Agriculture will encourage farmers to raise for export to China. "Especially the Ministry of Agriculture, how to have a contract or push with China or other stakeholders to provide warmth and encouragement, we will do." Mr. Sok Raden said that the subsequent opening of markets for Cambodian agricultural products exported to China is due to the good relations between the leaders of the two countries.
"Due to the flow of relations from the beginning of history since the time of the Chinese emperor, he has helped to build bridges, roads, and infrastructure. "Opportunity, which is a good relationship that provides a warm opportunity, especially the Chinese side has helped us a lot to see a lot of achievements, so we are happy with these achievements."
Article: Cambodia-China Friendship Radio Association of Higher Education Institutions Confirms Tax Preferences and Instructs Members to Follow Royal Government Decisions
(Phnom Penh): The Association of Higher Education Institutions, which has 123 member institutions, has instructed its members to follow the guidelines of the Royal Government regarding tax payment.
In its announcement on June 15, 2023, the association emphasized that the Royal Government of Cambodia pays high attention to the education sector and provides tax incentives to public and private educational institutions throughout the country.
According to the association, in order to alleviate the difficulties of educational institutions, the Royal Government has given preference to educational institutions to be exempt from income tax, income tax relief, minimum tax, value added tax and withholding tax on services. Money and dividends for an additional 5 years from 2024 to 2028. However, educational institutions are required to pay taxes on salaries, tax, rent, location, property tax and patent tax.
The association instructs its members to abide by the decisions of the Royal Government.
Please note that the Higher Education Association came out to confirm this after a school informed its parents to help pay 10 percent tuition tax. The demands of the above school are contrary to the Royal Government.
The General Department of Taxation of the Ministry of Economy and Finance has issued a statement stating that in accordance with the tax law promulgated on May 16, 2023, there is no tuition tax. In particular, the income tax rate of 20% is the responsibility of the educational institution, not the responsibility of the parents of the students. Therefore, there is no requirement for parents of students to pay 20% tax on tuition fee to the Royal Government per year.
