(Phnom Penh): According to the results of the vulture census on June 9, 2023, 131 vultures were recorded in three protected areas in Cambodia. This number is similar to the census results for the same period in 2022.
Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment Neth Pheaktra said that the Cambodian Vulture Conservation Group has conducted an annual vulture census to count the number of vultures in the wild in order to capture the number of endangered and endangered vultures. Take the necessary measures to protect and take care of this vulture for a long time to come.
According to the Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment, the June 9 vulture census is the second of five vulture censuses conducted annually by the Cambodian Vulture Conservation Group.
Ny Nai Ky, a vulture conservation officer with Natural Life in Cambodia, said that the March vulture census showed that there were about 105 vultures, a good figure for conservation work. Vultures in Cambodia show positive signs again.
According to Ny Nai Ky, the next vulture census by the Cambodian Vulture Conservation Group will be conducted for the third time during this June, the fourth in September and the fifth in December 2023, before the addition. Summarize and conclude how many vultures there will be in 2023.
According to the census, the situation of Cambodian vultures in 2022 has become more positive due to the efforts of the Cambodian Vulture Conservation Working Group, but it requires more attention from all parties to protect and conserve. This rare animal more.
The Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment confirmed that the census results in 2022 show that Cambodia has a total of 134 species of three vultures, including 71 gray vultures, 44 brown vultures and 19 fire vultures. The number of vultures has increased by 13 compared to 121 in 2011, which is good news for conservationists and for biodiversity in Cambodia and around the world.
The senior official of the Ministry of Environment requested all stakeholders to further protect and conserve this rare species of vulture by participating in protecting habitats, eliminating pollution, stopping traps and hunting, and stop disturbing activities by Others for the benefit of the ecosystem, the richness of biodiversity and the preservation of natural heritage for future generations.
