Abbas will visit the Chinese capital until Friday, his fifth official visit to the world's second-largest economy, Beijing said.
"Faced with a century of global change and a new evolution of the situation in the Middle East, China is willing to strengthen coordination and cooperation with the Palestinian side," Xi said during a welcoming ceremony at the Great Hall of the People.
"Today, we will jointly announce the establishment of a Sino-Palestinian strategic partnership, which will be a milestone in the history of bilateral relations," Xi added.
Abbas arrived in Beijing on Monday for talks with senior Chinese leaders, including President Xi and Prime Minister Li Qiang. The two sides plan to take this opportunity to discuss ways to strengthen ties and address long-term challenges to the Palestinian-Israeli relationship.
Beijing has sought to strengthen ties with the Middle East to challenge US influence, an effort that has angered Washington.
In December last year, President Xi visited Saudi Arabia, during which he met with Abbas and vowed to "work for a just and lasting first step towards a lasting solution to the Palestinian issue."
Beijing has since positioned itself as a mediator in the Middle East, helping to restore ties in March between Iran and Saudi Arabia, rivals in the region where the United States has been a major power broker for decades.
But finding a lasting solution to the Israeli-Palestinian tensions could be more difficult as peace talks between the two sides have stalled since 2014.
