Key remarks by Samdech Techo Hun Sen on the occasion of visiting nearly 20,000 workers in Phnom Penh
(Phnom Penh): On Tuesday, June 13, 2023, the Prime Minister of Cambodia Samdech Techo Hun Sen continued to meet with a total of nearly 20,000 workers from 11 factories operating in Phnom Penh. The meeting took place at a factory in Por Sen Chey district.
Following are the main contents of Samdech Techo Hun Sen's opportunity to visit nearly 20,000 workers in Phnom Penh:
* Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that the closeness between Samdech and workers because they consider Samdech as a relative in the family.
* Samdech Techo Hun Sen continues to call on landlords not to increase prices when workers raise wages.
* Samdech Techo Hun Sen sends Mr. Chhay Sinarith to the northeastern province to manage security after Vietnam erupts in violence
* Samdech Techo Hun Sen instructed the Ministry of Post and Telecommunications to talk with the telephone company to arrange the service at Samdech's location and organize various programs.
* Samdech Techo Hun Sen allows Mr. San Sochea to pay a courtesy call.
* Samdech Techo Hun Sen calls together to prevent factories from becoming places for drug use or distribution.
* Samdech Techo Hun Sen reminded that without the strategy of vaccination, it is not known that Cambodia has such a destiny.
* Samdech Techo Hun Sen continues to call for joint peacekeeping. Peace is an opportunity to do all kinds of things.
* Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that foreign opposition groups are campaigning to prevent people from going to the polls.
* Samdech Techo Hun Sen encourages Mr. Ry Kea, a former member of the National Rescue Party to continue to inspire this campaign.
* Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that the election law will be changed. Non-voters in 2023 will not be able to stand for election.
* Samdech Techo Hun Sen urges opposition commune / sangkat councils not to go to the polls after the election law is amended.
* Samdech Techo Hun Sen asks the President of the National Assembly and the Senate to assist the Royal Government in amending the law on both universal and non-universal elections.
