The association, which represents about 300 airlines, said lower inflation and lower fuel prices were indicative of the sustainability of strong air travel demand and declining spending.
CNA spoke to the presidents of airlines from the Asia-Pacific region at the three-day IATA annual summit from June 4 to 6 in Istanbul, Turkey.
The presidents said demand for air travel remained strong and the outlook for profit was strong, but warned that delays in aircraft acquisition could hamper the recovery and push up ticket prices. High.
Despite the influx of tourists returning to Japan, JAL's outbound traffic remains weak, at only about 50 percent of pre-epidemic levels.
The weakening yen and rising inflation have hampered the desire of Japanese people to travel abroad.
