Readers' views: Mekong politicians need to be able to distinguish between economic and political
Thai politicians should respect and value these migrant workers because without them, the Thai people would not be able to live a more comfortable and prosperous life. They need to respect those who support Thailand's livelihood and economic base, rather than inciting ultra-nationalist views on the economy for political gain.
The fact that we have to acknowledge is the virtue of Thai politicians like Thaksin and Yingluck Shinawatra. For them, despite resentment over coups, coups and deportations, they have never called on foreign governments to impose economic sanctions on Thailand.
Myanmar politician Aung San Suu Kyi has been detained both before and after the military coup, despite her incarceration and resolute commitment to the country. Despite the removal of the military from Myanmar's governing body, she has never called on foreign governments to destroy Myanmar's economy.
In terms of foreign policy and neighborliness, we can examine and note the Sino-Vietnamese relationship. The two countries are generally at war with each other tomorrow due to sovereignty disputes in the South China Sea. However, politicians of the two countries rarely clash their strong economic ties.
Bilateral trade between Vietnam and China reached 234.9 billion US dollars in 2022, double the four years from 2018 to 106.71 billion US dollars. Vietnam is China's largest trading partner among the 10 ASEAN member states and China's sixth largest trading partner in the world.
Instead of destroying economic ties, China and Vietnam can only further strengthen economic ties and ties between their people because they think that strong economic ties could serve as a barrier to peace without war. Politicians in the Mekong region must have ethics and virtues in distinguishing between economic and political.
For domestic politics, no matter how bitter the political competition, politicians must not ask foreigners to impose economic sanctions on their own country. For foreign relations, despite the different policies, the politicians of each country must be careful not to affect the livelihoods and well-being of the people in the region, because the economies of the countries in the Mekong region are interdependent. Very mutual.
This is a moral and ethical obligation that politicians in the Mekong region must uphold in promoting peace, stability, good neighborliness and common prosperity of our people.
By: Sim Virak, Strategic Advisor, Asian Vision Institute (AVI)
