The temple is called Abu Simbel in the south of Egypt and was built by King Ramses II. Since the fall of the new Egyptian Empire, the temple has been buried in the desert for more than 3,000 years. The West did not know about the temple, but in 1812 a Swiss explorer discovered the temple through the head of a large statue more than 20 meters high emerging from the sand.
Four years after the discovery, the Egyptian government carried out a project to remove sand from the temple grounds, but due to its remote location, the work was extremely slow and difficult, and the temple was not fully prepared. Not again until 1880.
In 1960, to help develop hydropower in Egypt, the United States helped the country build a dam at Aswan to hold the waters of Lake Nasir. After the construction of the dam, every year there are floods around the Nasor Lake, including the Abusimbel Temple.
