As of 2019, China has been the leader in development financing in Southeast Asia, the country's largest export partner, due to its prominence in infrastructure investment, Reuters reported. The Kovid-19 pandemic has hampered financing, meaning other lenders have overtaken China as they continue to provide financial assistance.
In 2021, China, the world's second-largest economy, will provide only 14 percent of the region's official development financing by 2021, down from 24 percent in 2015, the institute said.
A report by the Lowy Institute released on Tuesday also found a gap between China's commitment to the project and the country's actual disbursement, with Beijing financing only 40 percent between From 2015 to 2021.
According to Reuters, the slowdown in China's economy during the epidemic means that two of China's major policy banks have been slow to issue new loans abroad.
Chinese aid was also found to be concentrated across some countries, with Cambodia, Indonesia, Laos, Malaysia and Thailand receiving the largest share, while other lenders provided more balanced support than China across the region. And in areas other than infrastructure.
China remains the largest infrastructure financier in Southeast Asia, according to the report. China provided $ 4 billion a year between 2015 and 2021, while Japan provided $ 2.5 billion and the World Bank just over $ 1 billion over the same period. . According to Reuters.
