Watch the video: International analysis: Dam collapse disaster gives Russia a new weapon in the war in Ukraine (Video inside) (London): For weeks after the talk of Ukrainian drone strikes and strikes Crossing the border into Russian territory and Kiev's counter-offensive operation, it is now Russia's turn to divert attention and destabilize its enemies.
According to some military experts, Ukraine is unlikely to use the crossing to launch a large-scale offensive. But the collapse of the Kakhovka Dam will narrow Ukraine's options for launching small-scale attacks in the south, diverting Russian military attention and supporting large-scale attacks on other areas.
The disaster will make it easier for Russia to re-deploy troops to vulnerable parts of its military defenses, whether or not the dam collapse is a disaster that the Ukrainian authorities are facing, which could affect To focus on the main counter-operations this summer.
The collapse of the Kakhovka Dam seems to be in line with Russia's strategy to destroy key infrastructure in order to suffocate Ukraine's economy, deplete Ukraine and then evacuate the region. Apart from emergencies, the dam collapse will pose a risk to the environment, ecosystems, water supply and energy infrastructure.
However, at the moment, what the Kremlin is focusing on is how to prevent a successful Ukrainian counterattack, no matter which method is used. The destruction of the Kakhovka Dam could send a message that Russia still has the potential to escalate war at any time and also wants to send a message to Ukraine and the West that Moscow still has many unused options.
The statement came after Indonesia announced that ASEAN would hold its first joint military exercise in the South China Sea, as it would be the latest multilateral military exercise amid tensions as well as instability. Clearly growing in the region.
In a statement, the Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces described the outcome of the 20th ASEAN Chiefs of Staff Meeting in Bali, Indonesia on June 7, 2023, which reached a joint statement. The joint statement did not mention ASEAN joint military exercises in the South China Sea.
At the end of the meeting, the Indonesian Commander-in-Chief, who chaired the rotating meeting, outlined the Indonesian Army Commander-in-Chief's idea for joint military exercises in the South China Sea and sought the advice of the nine Commanders-in-Chief. Countries in which the Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, as well as the Commander-in-Chief of many other countries did not respond.
At the bilateral meeting, the Commander-in-Chief of the Indonesian Armed Forces again requested the opinion of the Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, in which the Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces responded:
* First, upon receipt of the invitation of the Commander-in-Chief of the Indonesian Armed Forces, we will assign a working group to study the joint military exercise.
* Second: After graduation, the Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces will request a decision from the Ministry of National Defense of Cambodia.
It should be noted that the issuance of the statement of the Commander-in-Chief of Cambodia is in response to the announcement of the Indonesian side on Thursday, announcing that ASEAN will hold its first joint military exercise in the South China Sea as it will The latest multilateral military exercise comes amid rising tensions and uncertainty in the region. This was released by the AFP news agency on Thursday, June 8, 2023.
The South China Sea is an area where some ASEAN members, such as Vietnam, the Philippines and Malaysia, have disputes with China, while ASEAN has been trying to push for a South China Sea Code of Conduct with Beijing. Remember for a long time that can be achieved. In particular, ASEAN solidarity is also facing a test in the face of US-China influence rivalries in the Asia-Pacific.
