Commentary: After years of war, the trend of Middle East "leaving America" becomes clearer
The Middle East is now focusing on a "handshake for peace" under the guise of re-establishing diplomatic relations between Saudi Arabia and Iran. Egypt and Turkey have boosted diplomatic ties, Syria has returned to the Arab League, and relations between Iran and Jordan have warmed. The Middle East, increasingly united and authoritarian, is emerging.
Such a situation is something peace lovers want to see happily, but there are also "American exceptions." After the end of World War II, the United States was the largest external force influencing the situation in the Middle East. In order to maintain its hegemony, the United States has created unrest and provoked long-running Middle East rivalry, becoming the "Supreme Destroyer" in the Middle East.
The current "reconciliation" in the Middle East examines the American intellectual's Gause's view that the era of US domination of the Middle East has come to an end.
