(Sihanoukville): Two Chinese nationals and a Cambodian man (translator) were arrested by the Sihanoukville Police Inspectorate for plotting to charge a Chinese woman with drug use and trafficking by placing two packages of drugs in a room. Hotel and make an appointment for the victim to enter the room and call the authorities.
However, the plan to want the victim to be arrested and imprisoned by the police did not succeed after the police questioned and examined the victim's urine and did not find any drugs.
According to the commissioner, the anti-drug force of the Sihanoukville Police Inspectorate, in cooperation with the Sangkat 4 police force, received information from a Chinese translator named Tra, that a Chinese woman was using Drugs and there are drugs in the room.
All three suspects are currently being held at the Sihanoukville Police Inspectorate for questioning and to build a case to be sent to the Sihanoukville Provincial Court for further drug search and processing.
To what extent do Cambodian migrant workers help the Thai economy?
(Phnom Penh): Migrant workers are the economic policies of the developed countries of the world, which need labor to support the production base of each country. The demand for labor, which refers to attracting workers from other countries, is likened to the old saying, "Hook it, then it will come," meaning that there is a need for a supplier, which is in line with the theory of economic development.
Currently, developed countries such as Japan, South Korea, Singapore, Malaysia, as well as moderately developed countries, also need labor from developing countries such as Vietnam, the Philippines, Cambodia, Laos and Myanmar.
The four most labor-intensive countries in ASEAN are Brunei, Malaysia, Singapore and Thailand.
The International Labor Organization (ILO) estimates the number of global migrant workers at 164 million, representing nearly 5% of the world's total labor force, of which about 12 million are from Southeast Asia and the Pacific.
Cambodia, on the other hand, has supplied its labor force through policies to develop an estimated 1.2 million migrant workers, most of them to Thailand.
According to the figures, Thai politicians are concerned about the repatriation of migrant workers working in Thailand. Most of those migrants are from Cambodia, Laos and Myanmar or Myanmar, which the International Organization for Migration (IoM) estimates has a total of about 5 million migrant workers in Thailand. At present, both legal and illegal.
Thailand: Economic gains or losses
The current situation in Thailand is facing a shortage of labor and the Thai economy is burdening the older Thai population. This trend makes Thai enterprises rely heavily on both skilled and unskilled labor from neighboring countries to develop their manufacturing enterprises.
Cambodia and Thailand experience: mutual benefit
Good relations between the Royal Government of Cambodia and the Royal Government of Thailand, where the presence of Cambodian workers helped the development of Thai industry, and Cambodia also received a lot of money from the transfer of money to support the families of Cambodian workers.
When Thailand fired illegal workers at that time, there were also protests from the owners of Thai manufacturing or industrial enterprises that there was a shortage of workers. And the shortage of workers will adversely affect the Thai production process. Meanwhile, research has found that foreign workers working in Thailand helped boost industrial productivity, which contributed to the 1% annual growth of the Thai economy, according to a study by the Thai Economic Institute.
Separately, Cambodia also benefits. If we look at the revenue figures for the national economy, Cambodia receives at least $ 3 billion a year from foreign migrant workers, according to the Ministry of Labor. In addition, Cambodian workers can learn technical experience or other professional work to apply in Cambodia. Currently, there are more than 1.3 million Cambodian workers working abroad, of which 1.2 million are working in Thailand.
