Royal School of Administration led 348 philanthropists to donate blood to the National Blood Transfusion Center to help patients
(Phnom Penh): The Royal School of Administration on June 6, 2023, led a total of 348 trainers, trainers, civil servants, contract officers, interns, students, officials and philanthropists who are members of the Public Service Commission. Participate in voluntary blood donation to the National Blood Transfusion Center to help patients and victims who need life-saving blood.
Mr. Youk Bunna, Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Public Works and Director of the Royal School of Administration, said that the Royal School of Administration has organized such programs three times from 2016 to 2022, with a total of 487 volunteers. For the fourth program, 348 people (86 women and 6 monks) donated blood.
Ms. Rath Sokkantya, a student of the Royal School of Administration, expressed her interest that when she did not donate blood, she had some worries, but when she donated blood, there were no problems. She hopes that the donated blood will save the lives of patients who need blood.
Venerable Thun Chantra, who also donated blood, said that donating blood is also a great merit because it is like giving alms to save the lives of others. Participating in blood donation, sacrificing to save the lives of others is also mentioned in the Cheatak scripture, when the bodhisattva sacrificed his heart to mix medicine to cure his mother, who was bitten by a demon, and also saved his merits. .
Regular blood donation not only does not harm the health, but it can also provide some important benefits to the benefactors, such as reducing cancer, reducing stress and cholesterol, reducing stroke. Cerebral arteries reduce blood pressure and excess iron, which affects the liver - pancreas, improve heart health, reduce weight and so on.
