Officials and conservationists call together to end destructive activities on nature and to eliminate natural resource crime
"Every living thing has a different role in nature, and the loss of something in human nature is the highest risk," he said. "Together, end the trap crisis, stop the trade and consumption of meat and wildlife products, to reconcile our relationship with nature for the long-term benefit of our country."
Mr. Seng Teak, Director of WWF Cambodia, added, “With the world-renowned reputation of archeology, Siem Reap is also a biodiversity-rich province, and much of it has yet to be discovered. No. A report by the regional WWF confirms the discovery of new species of plants and animals in Siem Reap by scientists. We believe there will be more recent discoveries in Cambodia's freshwater and marine forests. ”
He said the trap was threatening the revenue generated by the sale of carbon credits, a contribution to economic development projects and the prevention of eco-tourism, a source of income for rural people. Traps are like unexploded ordnance for wild animals because they kill indiscriminately. These traps have also destroyed businesses and economic opportunities for innocent families.
Tuy Serey Vathana, Program Manager of the Wildlife and Nature Coalition, said: “Therefore, stopping and ending the trapping will bring peace, which is the key to long-term benefits for wildlife, habitats and the well-being of the people. . Together we protect great biodiversity, improve community livelihoods and public health by promoting positive attitudes toward the goal of a "zero trap" in the future.
Kong Mony Chan, Director of the Mad Max Jolipit Foundation, added: "The sculptures in Angkor and the ancient temples of Cambodia show a lot of evidence of a balanced life between humans and wildlife. Cambodian ancestors have shown us that the conservation of nature and wildlife is part of a sustainable way of life, a culture of harmony between man and nature.
Now is the time for all of us to return to practice the right wisdom left by our Cambodian ancestors, which will help us preserve precious and rare wildlife and expand our ecological income opportunities. Tourism. "The Zero Trap Campaign is a great and timely reminder for all Cambodians to change their minds and continue to follow in the footsteps of our ancestors by participating in the rescue and conservation of our country's rare and precious wildlife."
"Eating wildlife meat is a major cause of trapping, shooting and pollution," said Bou Rossak, director of Natural Life Cambodia. Thousands of traps are being set to catch wildlife if this illegal activity continues to grow our forest and become a wildlife-free forest. "The public, local authorities, organizations and relevant institutions must work together to prevent trapping in the forest and all forms of wildlife trade."
