Survivors of the genocidal regime want to cite the history of Cambodia, for example, "If Samdech Techo Hun Sen had not fled to find a force to liberate the people from the Khmer Rouge regime, what would have been history?"
Facts and histories cannot be falsified, but the reality of reality can be deceived by political tendencies that deviate from the reality of reality. In this short article, I question whether the historical role of Prime Minister Hun Sen in the fight against the Khmer Rouge has fallen victim to political bias based on reality.
Figurative history is a chapter of history that is anticipated and fabricated by one or more historical events that occur differently from actual events. The parable gives an important idea to consider the history of Cambodia and the history of Prime Minister Hun Sen's struggle against the Khmer Rouge.
No such metaphorical history has been written about Cambodia under the Khmer Rouge regime. If there is a metaphorical history for Cambodia, it would be beneficial to think about what Cambodia would be like if events related to the overthrow and destruction of the Khmer Rouge movement did not occur or occur. In a different situation.
An in-depth discussion of figurative history can lead to many important questions. For example: What if Prime Minister Hun Sen did not flee Cambodia in 1977? What if there were no anti-Khmer Rouge forces? And if Samdech Prime Minister Hun Sen did not create or implement a win-win policy?
