(Phnom Penh): Mr. Hang Samoeun, Second Vice Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management and Advisor to the Prime Minister of Cambodia Samdech Techo Hun Sen, met with Mr. Yan Yun, Deputy Director General of the Department of Policy, Law and Regulation of the Ministry of Disaster Management. People's Republic of China at the National Committee for Disaster Management.
The meeting was also attended by the Secretary-General, Deputy Secretary-General and Technical Officer of the NCCC this morning, June 2, 2023, as the Chinese side continues to pay an official visit to Cambodia. From May 31 to June 3, 2023.
During the meeting, Mr. Hang Samoeun, on behalf of His Excellency Dr. Kun Kim, First Vice President, thanked and appreciated the delegation of the Ministry of Emergency Management of the People's Republic of China for the strong bilateral relations and close cooperation between the two institutions. Cambodia and China, in particular, achieved the signing of a Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) on October 29, 2021.
* First, the exchange of arrangements for official visits and exchange of experiences for leaders and technicians to the Ministry of Emergency Management of the People's Republic of China.
* Second: Capacity building (software), training and capacity building of officials on work, disaster risk reduction and effective emergency response.
* Third: Institutional (hardware) Establishment of the Emergency Operations Center (EDC) will be equipped with equipment in the DAC, Cambodia, the establishment of disaster relief warehouses due to the Cambodian national standard Establishment of the Disaster Training Center Disaster in Cambodia, equipping means and materials in Cambodian disaster search and rescue operations.
He emphasized that the visit was aimed at strengthening ties and deepening the level of cooperation between the two Cambodian and Chinese institutions, both at the technical level. Leading the progress of emergency management, especially the emergency management legal system of the People's Republic of China.
Launch of the ASEAN-China Center for High Operations, Emergency Management, Implementation Plan for the China-Cambodia Friendship City Project, and Joint Development of a Disaster Risk Reduction Booklet for Children and Youth in Cambodia.
A 90-year-old woman in Kien Svay district, Kandal province, filed a complaint for intervention from Samdech Techo Hun Sen after her children and grandchildren stole a plot of land and were evicted
(Kandal): A 90-year-old woman living in Chheu Teal village, Chheu Teal commune, Kien Svay district, Kandal province, named Doung Moeun, has filed a petition for intervention from Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, and Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen. And grandchildren stole land plans and forced her to dismantle the house from the land size of 10 meters by 110 meters.
In the petition for intervention from Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittipritthbandit, which was received by Fresh News on June 2, 2023, Ms. Doung Moeun described that she received a donation from the state of 10 meters of land. 110 meters to build a house to live in until 2001, she built a house 5 meters by 7 meters made of wood and tiles to stay.
In 2009, she built another house, 5 meters by 7 meters, made of stone (above the house made of wood), then saw that her daughter Tey Nem was poor, her husband was dead and she had three small children. In charge of just renting a house size of 5 meters by 7 meters, made of stone, temporary residence to engage in the business of selling and settling for a living.
Through this letter, Mrs. Doung Moeun requested the intervention of Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittipritthbandit to help find justice so that she can get her full right to return the land. 10 more restaurants in Siem Reap announced not to sell wild meat dishes
(Siem Reap): By promoting awareness of the value of wildlife and the legal challenges of bringing wildlife meat to cook and sell to customers, 10 restaurant owners in Siem Reap have announced that Sell food made from wild animals to customers, even if customers order and pay a high price.
With the launch of the Zero Trap Campaign in Siem Reap, in addition to the above restaurant owners, people and the public from all walks of life have pledged to bring their awareness of the benefits of natural resources and wildlife to families, relatives and Its friends, too.
So far, more than 96 restaurants in 10 provinces, including Kratie, Stung Treng, Preah Vihear, Ratanakkiri, Mondulkiri, Pursat, Kampong Speu, Koh Kong and Siem Reap, have vowed not to sell wildlife food to participate in the cut. Reduce poaching and killing wildlife in protected areas and wildlife conservation.
Speaking at a press conference on the second phase of the Zero Trap Campaign in Siem Reap on the afternoon of June 1, 2023 at the Siem Reap Provincial Department of Environment, Mr. Net Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment and Chairman of the Zero Trap Campaign, said: In the second phase of the Zero Trap campaign, at least 10 restaurants in Siem Reap have vowed not to sell wildlife meat. ”
The senior official emphasized that the participation of the restaurant owners is an important part in preventing the killing of wild animals and also to reduce the trapping in natural areas. The participation of the people is an important mechanism in the conservation of wildlife for the longevity of future generations.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra added that according to the survey, wildlife species in the Siem Reap Provincial Geographical Protected Area have been declining alarmingly. Some species of wildlife, such as rattlesnakes, vultures, bears, deer, birds, are endangered and are listed as rare and endangered on the IUCN Red List.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra expressed his pride and congratulations to all the restaurant owners who volunteered not to sell wild meat dishes, emphasizing that "it is the most valuable and effective participation because the restaurant is a place "Distribute and create a significant wildlife trade, and if there is a sale of wildlife dishes, it is part of encouraging trapping and hunting."
He also called on restaurant owners in other provinces with more protected areas to participate more effectively for the protection and conservation of wildlife.
Mr. Pheaktra said that if there is no demand, there is no supply. Therefore, to prevent wildlife hunting, wildlife trafficking, please those who like to eat wild animals, give up the habit of eating wild animals and do not believe that wild animals are a tonic or medicine, but on the contrary, it can pose a high risk to your health. Brothers and sisters, because the meat of wild animals is not properly examined and embedded with deadly viruses that can kill our lives and cause other infections.
According to Siem Reap Deputy Governor Khim Finang: “Eating wild animals is not a bad thing, especially in Siem Reap, which is an important tourist destination in Cambodia. "We need to end the trap and stop eating wild animals."
More than 500 people march to launch the second Zero Trap Campaign in Siem Reap with hopes for wildlife and the elimination of all kinds of traps
(Siem Reap): Officials of the Ministry of Environment, Siem Reap Provincial Authority, civil society organizations, journalists and relevant departments, as well as more than 500 students this morning, June 2, 2023, marched to open the second zero trap campaign in Siem Reap. .
The Opening Ceremony of the Second Zero Trap Campaign in Siem Reap Province was led by Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment and Chairman of the Zero Trap Campaign and Koh Kong provincial leaders at all levels.
Siem Reap is the 10th province to host the Zero Trap campaign. The second phase of the Zero Trap Campaign will run for five months from March 3 to July 2023 and will be implemented in five provinces: Kampong Speu, Pursat, Koh Kong, Siem Reap, Battambang and Pailin.
The second Trap Zero Campaign in Siem Reap aims to reduce and prevent the number of traps in protected areas in Siem Reap, as well as in protected areas in Cambodia, and to cultivate a greater love for wildlife in Among the public.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra emphasized that the Zero Trap Campaign aims to further raise public awareness of the consequences of wildlife trapping, wildlife trafficking and wildlife consumption, including risks to public health and loss of resources. Nature, too. The campaign will also continue to spread the word about Cambodia's need for wildlife conservation and Cambodia's rich natural resources to support sustainable development.
