The State Department says Washington has stopped providing data on the status and location of nuclear missiles, as well as means for launching them to the Russian side. In addition, the United States will no longer provide telematics data, which is data collected during the flight of missiles around intercontinental ballistic missiles (ICBMs) and submarine-launched ballistic missiles (SLBMs). Continue to inform in advance about these missile tests.
"The move is in response to Russia's continued violation of the New START Treaty," the State Department said in a statement. The United States has informed Russia in advance of these actions and also indicated Washington's intention and readiness to overthrow them and to fully implement the New START Treaty if Russia agrees to return. "Respect the treaty."
Russian President Vladimir Putin did not formally withdraw Russia from the New START Treaty, but announced in February this year that it would suspend its involvement, leading to a final agreement on nuclear weapons control between Russia and Russia. And the United States is at risk.
