The announcement to reduce rice exports by Vietnam will aim to boost high-quality rice exports, ensure domestic food security, protect the environment and adapt to climate change.
By 2025, 60 percent of Vietnam's rice exports will go to Asia, 22 percent to Africa, 7 percent to the United States, 4 percent to the United States and 3 percent to Europe, the document said.
On the other hand, the soil quality in Vietnam seems to be deteriorating due to the country cultivating rice three times a year and using a lot of pesticides and fertilizers, which has damaged the quality of the soil for the last 10 years. Plant rice three times a year, then twice a year and switch to other crops that get a similar price for rice.
He added that changing Vietnam's rice export policy could provide both opportunities for Cambodia and challenges for Cambodia. However, he said that Cambodia should not follow in the footsteps of Vietnam, farming three times a year and using high fertilizers. Rice cultivation should be maintained twice a year and focus on cultivating high quality rice that is more expensive than Vietnam's rice exports.
He said that this will make Cambodia's rice cultivation sustainable and help the environment.
On the other hand, turning to farming three times a year requires a lot of involvement from stakeholders to support, encourage and send experts to train farmers, which can cost a lot of money.
According to Kan Kunthy, most of Vietnam's rice is exported. While Cambodia exports most of the high-value luxury rice such as fragrant rice, organic rice, steamed rice and white rice.
Please be informed that in the first three months of 2023, Cambodia exported more than 170,000 tons of rice to 42 target markets with a budget of more than 126 million dollars. Meanwhile, rice exports amounted to 1.56 million tons, earning 390.12 million US dollars.
