Vietnam is the world's third largest rice exporter after India and Thailand.
The measure is aimed at "boosting the export of high-quality rice, ensuring domestic food security, protecting the environment and adapting to climate change," Reuters reported, citing a Vietnamese government document dated May 26. "
A Ho Chi Minh City-based rice trader told Reuters on Saturday: "Even though Vietnam's farmland is declining due to climate change, some farmers are switching to other crops and shrimp farming. "Also, this strategy seems too panic."
Some farmers in the Mekong Delta are turning parts of their fields into orchards of mango, grapefruit, jackfruit and durian, but most still rely on rice, he said.
By 2025, 60 percent of Vietnam's rice exports will go to Asia, 22 percent to Africa, 7 percent to the United States, 4 percent to the Middle East and 3 percent to Europe, the document said.
Rice exports from Vietnam in the first four months of this year rose 40.7 percent year on year to 2.9 million tons, according to Vietnamese government customs data.
