Othy, Director of the International Institute for Rural Rehabilitation of Cambodia, said that the workshop focused on the basic concepts of the food rights system through the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Farmers and Indigenous Peoples. Others living in rural areas, as well as voluntary guidelines for food and nutrition systems. The workshop also examined and discussed in depth the specific context of the food system in Cambodia.
Mr. Chea Samnang, Deputy Chairman of the Council for Agricultural and Rural Development, stated that the Royal Government recognizes the importance of the right to food as a fundamental right of every person and that every Cambodian has the right to safe food. Adequate quality and nutrition to meet their dietary requirements, as set out in the International Covenant on Human Rights and an essential component of the Sustainable Development Goals.
Mr. Chea Samnang emphasized: "The food system and access rights are not just about food production, but also about fair distribution, access to nutritious food and reasonable food prices." "Everyone is satisfied, regardless of their socio-economic status."
He also stressed that the implementation of food storage systems, efficient transportation, promoting responsible consumer behavior and supporting initiatives that divert excess food to those in need, we need to cut. Reduce food waste to ensure that no Cambodian is starving.
On that occasion, Mr. Sok Silo, Secretary General of the Council for the Rehabilitation of Agriculture and Rural Development, also highlighted that on this planet, every day, more than 7.2 billion people need food, and by 2050 at least. Yes, more than 2 billion people will need more food.
He asked if the world at that time could have as much food as it does today if we did not change our food system. Currently, the global food system is facing many challenges. The Council for Agricultural and Rural Development has worked to strengthen multi-sectoral mechanisms to address challenges in Cambodia's food system and to ensure food rights.
