He noted that tobacco is a risk factor for non-communicable diseases such as cancer, cardiovascular disease, lung disease, affect all organs and affect the economy, environment and society. Fresh tobacco leaves also contain nicotine and latex, which can affect farmers' health, such as fresh tobacco disease with symptoms such as vomiting, nausea, dizziness, headache, sweating, chills, abdominal pain, diarrhea, shortness of breath and more.
Tobacco farmers are exposed daily to tobacco debris and other chemical toxins. Research by the World Health Organization shows that tobacco farmers who grow and harvest tobacco can absorb up to 50 cigarettes a day. In addition, tobacco farmers often bring home harmful substances on their bodies, clothing, or footwear that endanger their families, especially children.
At the same time, tobacco cultivation requires the use of strong pesticides and fertilizers, which contribute to soil degradation. Chemicals enter the atmosphere in polluted water, lakes, rivers and drinking water. The soil used for growing tobacco is less fertile, making it less productive for other food crops. Therefore, the World Health Organization encourages States Parties to switch from tobacco crops to food crops.
The Royal Government, under the right leadership, with a long-term vision and realism of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, has strived to reduce tobacco use by enacting laws on tobacco products and other regulatory documents. Subsequently.
