Health minister calls for end to smoking, which causes premature births, lung cancer and non-communicable diseases, leading to loss of health and family economy
(Phnom Penh): Tobacco has many effects on public health that cause non-communicable diseases such as cancer, cardiovascular disease, lung disease and affect all organs, including the economy, environment and society, Professor Mam Bunheng, Minister Health and chairman of the Tobacco Products Control Committee has called for an end to smoking to prolong life.
He made the remarks during the celebration of World No Tobacco Day 2023 under the theme "We need food, not tobacco", focusing on promoting sustainable food crops instead of tobacco. Held at the Ministry of Health on May 29, 2023.
World No Tobacco Day, a major event designated by the World Health Organization since 1987, is celebrated annually on May 31 to commemorate the serious effects of tobacco products on economic health. Environment and society, and call on all States Parties and stakeholders to work together to promote the cessation of tobacco use and to prevent current and future generations of tobacco use.
"Tobacco causes lung cancer, premature birth, loss of health and family economy, especially to understand that tobacco is not a food and can not be a substitute for food," he said. "So you have to save money to buy food, more specifically, there is no cigarette smoke that harms others."
He strongly hopes that our people will be more involved, we must understand that tobacco can not replace food, run out of money and affect the health of ourselves and our families, especially women who are smoking. Pregnancy can cause premature birth.
