Discover India where the King is on a state visit (Video inside) (New Delhi): On the morning of Monday, May 29, 2023 His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni, King of the Kingdom of Cambodia, wishes to pay a state visit to India.
According to the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, His Majesty King Bhumibol Adulyadej is on a three-day visit to India from May 29 to 31, 2023. Of Indian President Duterte Padimir Moo.
According to the Cambodian Ministry of Foreign Affairs, during the visit, Indian President Drew Padimu and Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi will hold a royal court hearing for His Majesty King Bhumibol Adulyadej. Norodom Sihamoni, on which occasion, bilateral and multilateral cooperation will be discussed. Here are some important things to know about India:
1. Curriculum Vitae
2. Important dates in Indian history
Indian culture has been around for 4,500 years. Indian society is defined by classes in a very restrictive society. Family values are very important in Indian tradition.
Marriage is considered an important part of life, so divorce rates are very low. As of 2001, only 1.6 percent of Indian women were divorced, but this figure has risen due to women's education and economic independence. Child marriage has become commonplace, especially in rural areas. Many women get married before the age of 18, which is their legal marriage age.
4. International and military relations
Since gaining independence in 1947, India has maintained friendly relations with many nations. In the 1950s, India took on the role of supporter of leading the independence claims of countries in Asia and Africa under European colonization.
Recently, India has played an influential role in ASEAN, SAARC and WTO. India has long been a member and supporter of the United Nations. There are more than 55,000 Indian troops and police in 35 security operations across the four continents.
Since gaining independence, India has pursued a socialist orientation with strict control over the private sector, such as foreign trade and foreign direct investment. However, since 1991, India has opened up its markets by reforming its economy and easing state control over foreign trade and investment.
