Government agencies and stakeholders meet to prepare a legal document setting the minimum age for buying alcohol in Cambodia
As part of the second round of discussions, the results of a joint study conducted by AVI and MM4A on the perceptions of young people in Cambodia on the minimum legal age to purchase LPA were presented and shared. Research shows that 85 percent of those surveyed support the introduction of a legal age limit for LPA in Cambodia, and 73 percent say the legal age should be 18 or older.
Consensus among Cambodian youth surveyed reflects concerns over the issue of underage drinking, with 33% indicating that they were under the age of 18 and were under the influence of alcohol or alcoholic beverages. In some cases, children as young as 6 years old. In addition, 63% indicated that they bought alcoholic beverages in small depots and non-commercial retail outlets (small shops and one-stop shops).
On that occasion, Mr. Phan Oun emphasized, "Today is a workshop. Today's workshop is a great sharing and discussion, leading to the strengthening and strong consensus in the law on sub-decree on the minimum legal age to buy LPA for "Cambodia with an appropriate age in the form of legal documents."
General Department of Consumer Protection, Competition and Anti-Fraud "KPR" Fully support this strategy and have the pleasure of directing the draft sub-decree of the relevant ministries and achieving this important event in protecting minors from alcohol and alcoholic beverages. .
