(Phnom Penh): As we all know, the effects of radiation are very catastrophic for human health, animals and nature.
5. 1957 Windscale was the first British nuclear reactor to be built in the late 1940s in northwestern England. On October 10, 1957, the staff carrying out regular maintenance work there observed an increase in temperature. Further examination revealed that the reactor core, which was filled with uranium, was on fire.
The staff at the process control room made a mistake while trying to alleviate the problem, and in the morning the reactor core warmed up to almost 4,000 degrees Celsius, only 1,000 degrees Celsius, and the nucleus would melt. As radiation began to flood the nuclear power plant, news of the disaster spread to the outside world.
The incident was not immediately recognized, which led to lengthy resolution efforts and delayed damage management. On March 31, 1979, factory workers were able to resolve the issue by eliminating the possibility of melting the reactor core. No one was killed or injured.
Reactor 2 was so badly damaged that it could not be reused. Reactor 1 is still operational, but it was shut down in 2019. The disaster has also undermined the US public's trust in nuclear power, sparking a series of protests and raising awareness of the importance of state and city preparedness for emergencies. Up.
3. Russia's 1957 radiation disaster The Soviet Union set up hundreds of secret sites after World War II, most of which were built in a hurry and without much care to increase stockpiles. Their nuclear weapons. One of them is the Mayak nuclear power plant in Ozyorsk, Russia.
2. Japan's 2011 nuclear disaster The Fukushima nuclear disaster in 2011 was the world's second-worst nuclear disaster with a magnitude of seven. The power plant was built between 1971 and 1979 and has a total of six reactors, but only the first, second and third reactors were operating at the time of the disaster.
In the first few days, the power outage caused the cooling system to malfunction. This caused the nuclear fuel poles in each reactor to overheat and even to some extent. As a result, there is also the release of radioactive substances to the outside.
There were subsequent explosions and fires at the three reactors, but no one was killed during the incident. At least 16 employees were injured and hundreds more were exposed to radiation. Many people around the 20-kilometer radius of the emergency zone were evacuated, with 47,000 people displaced.
During one test, an explosion occurred, causing the flames to ignite and to emit radiation for nine days. The radiation death toll rose to 31 and about 4,000 died prematurely.
