The National Committee for Disaster Management and the World Bank hold a consultative workshop on the verification of the results of the assessment of preparedness for disaster response in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): The National Committee for Disaster Management in collaboration with the World Bank on the morning of May 25, 2023 organized a consultative workshop on the verification of the results of the report on the assessment of preparedness for emergency response in Cambodia.
The ceremony was presided over by Mr. Hang Samoeun, Second Vice President of the National Committee for Disaster Management and a personal advisor to Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, on behalf of His Excellency Dr. Kun Kim, Senior Minister, First Deputy Chairman (NCCC). M).
On behalf of Dr. Kun Kim, Mr. Hang Samoeun said that while the world is going through many risks posed by natural and man-made causes of disasters. In particular, in Cambodia, natural disasters occur every year, destroying property and lives, such as: floods, rain, wind, lightning strikes, droughts, river bank collapses and epidemics.
He added that human-caused accidents have also recently occurred, such as: construction accidents in addition to fires, traffic accidents and some other accidents. Disaster risks continue to occur and affect the process of socio-economic activities.
Mr. Hang Samoeun continued that with the cooperation and support of the World Bank, the second consultation workshop was organized for the purpose of final verification of the report on the in-depth assessment of the capacity of preparation and Emergency response in Cambodia, which will decide on the input to be included in the 19 investment plans that are planned to be issued to meet the needs of effective emergency preparedness and response.
The Second Vice-Chairperson (NCCC) strongly hopes that she will gain a clear understanding of the strengths of Cambodia's emergency preparedness and response capabilities and the opportunities for action. To improve.
Ability to more effectively prepare for and respond to emergencies. In this regard, the outcome of this consultation workshop will prepare an investment plan that is tailored to specific needs and will be used as a basis for mobilizing technical and financial resources to support the implementation of disaster risk management activities. In Cambodia, be more efficient and increase resources in local communities.
It should also be noted that in the workshop, participants can review and provide input to confirm the results of the study. Disaster preparedness and response capacity in Cambodia.
