(Phnom Penh): Mr. Kong Vibol, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Director General of the General Department of Taxation, said that the new tax law, which was just promulgated in May 2023, does not intend to impose additional burdens or increase rates. Taxes to the taxpayer.
Mr. Kong Vibol made this statement in a special live program on the tax law on May 25, 2023.
The Director General of Taxation emphasized that the drafting of the new tax law has been modernized, improved and clearer, as well as the central government reform policy since 2015, and has not increased the tax rate. While most of the content has been retained, only some of the wording has been adapted to be consistent with other laws.
"Overall, the new fiscal law would like to inform the public as well as the taxpayers that we do not impose additional burdens on the taxpayers, only to facilitate and provide some clauses," he said. "To be clearer, in line with international standards and in line with some of the agreements we have signed overlapping taxes."
He added that the Royal Government has taken care of and carefully considered the taxpayers, from encouraging investors to providing incentives in various fields, that is, not to tax, cause economic conflicts or obstruction. No business.
"I would like to reiterate that this law is not new, it collects cluttered regulations that confuse taxpayers and sometimes tax officials," he said. "This is a work that is being modernized to make it easier, better, clearer and more consistent."
In order for the public and taxpayers to better understand the new tax law in the future, the General Department of Taxation will begin to disseminate widely to explain in detail to the public as well as all taxpayers to better understand the new tax. This.
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Prohm Vichet Akhara, Deputy Secretary General and Spokesman of the Constitutional Council, further explained that the Candlelight Party and the Khmer National Unity Party will not be able to participate in the 7th National Assembly election.
The Constitutional Council has decided to reject a complaint filed by the National Election Committee (NEC) by the National Election Committee (NEC) on the non-registration of candidates for the National Assembly election by the Candlelight Party and the Khmer National Unity Party. This decision was made after the opening of the plenary meeting chaired by Dr. M. Chhun Lim on the morning of May 25, 2025.
The spokesman said that in the legal position of the Constitutional Council, through the plenary session, received the complaints of the Candlelight Party and the Khmer National Unity Party as the correct form. But what is important is that the law does not respond because the Constitutional Council mainly reviews the decision of the NEC whether the decision on both parties is correct.
As a matter of law, the Constitutional Council weighed in on the subject matter of the two parties' submissions to the Constitutional Council and weighed in on the NEC's decision. In this verification, it is seen that the NEC has complied with Articles 27 and 31 of the Law on the Election of the National Assembly, which is in accordance with the spirit of this article.
The Constitutional Council examines the conditions of the legal form and determines that the lawsuits of both parties are contrary to the law because the NEC has complied with Articles 27 and 31 of the Law on the Election of Members of the National Assembly.
According to a press release from the Constitutional Council, read by Mr. Taing Ratana, Secretary General and spokesman for the Constitutional Council, after the meeting, the Constitutional Council considered the two lawsuits to be legal but rejected them illegally.
Separately, 18 political parties have been officially recognized by the NEC on the registration of political parties for the 7th National Assembly election to be held on July 23, 2023 after Those parties meet all the conditions of the election law.
