(Phnom Penh): Deputy Chairman of the Working Group and the second candidate of the Phleung Tien Party in Pursat province, Mr. Ing Hok Sreang, decided to leave the Phleung Tien Party and join the political life with the Cambodian People's Party.
The leader, who has traveled with the opposition for many years, said the reason he left the party was because of corruption and partisanship within the party.
Mr. Ing Hok Sreang emphasized, "I have seen in the Candlelight Party that there is controversy, corruption, partisanship, so I can not stand and live with the Candlelight Party anymore."
With the decision to leave the Candlelight Party, Mr. Hok Sreang also requested to join the political life with the Cambodian People's Party.
The request of the opposition leader was warmly welcomed by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian People's Party.
It should be noted that in the last period, many leaders of the Candlelight Party are rushing to leave the Candlelight Party and go to live with the Cambodian People's Party. They left under the pretext of losing faith in the leadership of the Candlelight Party and under the pretext that corruption and partisanship did not value the party's fighters.
Cambodia's Perspective: Integrating Transitional Justice into Education
The ECCC was initiated in 1997 and convicted three senior Khmer Rouge leaders. Although the ECCC has closed the judiciary, the rest of the work begins with a focus on disseminating the tribunal's legacy, especially in the field of education.
Therefore, I can connect that if a father wants to marry a child to a villager who is a former Khmer Rouge, he will be relieved. Because he also does not want his children to be ridiculed for marrying those responsible or committing crimes.
But the ECCC cannot and should not do this alone. As a partner in the's Southeast Asia University Partnership for Peace program of the German Civil Service Agency, the ECCC will incorporate this tribunal legacy into Cambodia's higher education program.
There is a saying, "Raising a child well requires a village." The ECCC is like a child in a peaceful village with a population of 16 million Cambodians. Therefore, it is time for the Cambodian people to unite and understand clearly what this heritage looks like and how it is useful in our daily lives.
