Samdech Techo said, "Be careful, 100 years from now or 50 years from now, the next person who receives the link from us will not follow the rules, which I would like to ask to be especially careful about the statues and statues."
Samdech Techo Hun Sen continued to warn that in terms of ancient clothing, it must be clearly named at any time, because art has different forms according to the period, such as the mountain kingdom, Chenla kingdom, Angkor period, Chaktomuk period, Longvek period, Udong period, colonial period and so on. .
Samdech Techo Prime Minister does not believe in the cultural change of any nation due to coercion or cultural aggression by other nations, but what is worrying is that the cultural change is done by Voluntary or due to misunderstanding, inaccuracy, inaccuracy or original inheritance form.
"Cultural aggression, I do not believe, I still claim there is no cultural aggression, but the most frightening thing is that this voluntary acceptance is something to be careful about," he said.
Samdech Techo recalled the period when France colonized Cambodia for more than 90 years, but France could not change Cambodian culture because the Cambodian people did not want to receive French culture, in which Khmer pagodas are still preserved. Literature has survived and some scholars have also helped to preserve ancient Khmer culture.
Samdech mentioned some cases of pagodas when building Buddha statues, using incorrect injection molds according to the rules, so be careful and pay attention to the rules of building something that is purely related to Khmer culture.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen added that Samdech knows that everyone wants to seek justice, but if a country submits a matter to the Prime Minister to solve, and when he sees the story, he always sees in which province. Send it to that province to solve, and after a while, there are issues almost every day, so I put the blog down at once, and then Samdech will start blogging gradually because there are too many things.
At the same time, Samdech urged the owners of mobile phone companies to install as many antennas as possible to provide adequate telephone services to the people.
