(Colombo): Sinopec has signed a contract to invest in Sri Lanka's retail fuel market as the crisis-stricken country seeks to ease pressure on currency reserves, the president's office said on Monday. Its a small dollar.
Under the new agreement, Chinese company Sinopec will be licensed for 20 years to operate 150 gas stations and will be able to invest in 50 new gas stations, Reuters reported.
A statement issued by the Sri Lankan presidential office said Sinopec could start operations within 45 days of issuing the license.
Sri Lanka is facing its worst financial crisis since gaining independence from the United Kingdom in 1948, with just $ 2.7 billion in foreign exchange reserves at the end of April.
Sinopec can now import fuel without relying on the dollars of Sri Lanka's local banks, the statement said.
"This development brings hope for a stable and reliable supply of fuel, boosts the country's energy sector and provides consumer guarantees," the statement added.
